														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4852341">
									

<p>Or, obvious solution. Periods of intermittent rainfall. It IS an oceanic world, after all, and that could be enough to give him fresh water. Would that work to solve the water problem? And in the meantime, maintaining a diverse undersea ecosystem, while leaning away from the manufactured &quot;paradise&quot; aspects of the world. Trade and communication, yes, but less emphasis on the world being &quot;perfect.&quot;</p>
<p>Steering away from the mechanics for a moment, what I want to focus on in this story is more the power dynamic that can arise from three different species trapped on a world where one of them has a distinct advantage, one is relatively free to travel - being a winged creature - and one is borderline helpless. I wanted to explore the psychology of the creatures by putting the human at a distinct disadvantage in this scenario, adding to the horror of the situation.</p>
<p>That being said, I need some days to let the psychology run its course. I plan to have a cetacean who's initially friendly and caring, but who desperately misses her pod, and uses the human to compensate for company. They'd travel together for some days, with the cetacean realizing that the scientist is dependent on her to provide food, leading to (in her mind) a benevolent power imbalance and letting her fill the void that came from being separated from her pod.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>This gets complicated when a flying arachnid appears, and the scientist interacts with it as well, despite some initial trepidation. I intend for the cetacean to become jealous and possessive, seeing the human as &quot;hers&quot; and resenting that the power imbalance with the arachnid isn't as pronounced, as it can at least leave and fly away from her for a while, even if it's still dependent on food for her - it's a small amount of freedom, but enough to irk the cetacean and drive her towards madness.</p>
<p>This would come to a head when the cetacean would encourage the insect to &quot;pull its own weight,&quot; concocting a scheme whereby she drains its strength while encouraging it to take on scouting duties while she stays with &quot;her&quot; human. The insect gets weaker as a result, and eventually crashes into the ocean, where it's left (barely) ambiguous as to whether the cetacean kills it while retrieving it, leaving her alone with &quot;her&quot; human.</p>
<p>From that point on, the human is the utter captive of the cetacean, and she starts treating him poorly - giving him half-chewed food that she &quot;deigns&quot; to allow him to have, keeping him up and not allowing him to sleep, and psychologically torturing him by emphasizing that he can live or die at her whim. He only manages to escape when he finds one of the few tiny islands on the world, giving him a brief reprieve, though now he's under siege from the cetacean. He manages to escape when one of the teleportation orbs filled with mist washes up on shore, and he mixes it with water from his own ocean, allowing him to teleport home, leaving the cetacean the sole, lonely inhabitant of a former, now-empty paradise.</p>
</div>
</div>
